```java
class Worker implements Runnable {
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public void run() {
        while (running) {
            doWork();
        }
    }

    void stop() {
        running = false;   // single write, single read on the other side
    }
}
```

This is exactly the case `volatile` is built for: one thread writes a flag once, another thread reads it repeatedly, and there is no read-modify-write on either side. Without `volatile`, the worker thread could cache `running` in a register or CPU cache and never observe the write, looping forever.

```java
class Counter {
    private volatile int count = 0;

    void increment() {
        count++;   // read, add, write — volatile fixes none of the three steps' atomicity
    }
}
```

`volatile` makes every read of `count` see the latest write, but `count++` is still read-modify-write, and `volatile` provides zero atomicity for that sequence. Two threads can both read the same value before either writes back, and one increment is lost — the exact bug `volatile` looks like it should prevent, but doesn't.

```java
class Counter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    void increment() {
        count.incrementAndGet();   // the read-modify-write itself is now atomic
    }
}
```

Swapping `volatile int` for `AtomicInteger` fixes exactly the gap `volatile` left: `incrementAndGet()` performs the read-modify-write as one atomic step internally, using a compare-and-swap loop, instead of relying on the caller to protect it. Same visibility guarantee as `volatile`, plus the atomicity `volatile` never provided.
